support.reconciliation:
Humanism has no scripture and no classical commentary, so there is no traditional reconciliation to cite. Its counted texts are revisable consensus statements, and the bodies that issue them say which text is current. The Humanists International General Assembly adopted the Declaration of Modern Humanism in 2022, replacing the 2002 Amsterdam Declaration, which had itself followed the 1952 text. The American Humanist Association has declared Manifestos I and II historic and superseded by Manifesto III (sources/corpus.md). The Minimum Statement (1996) is the one text every member organisation must accept.

On killing in personal life, the support researcher found no passages on both sides. No counted text names killing, and none permits, commands or approves a private killing of any class of person, so there is nothing within K1 itself for the tradition to reconcile. This is subject to the separate counter-evidence search. The principle carried through every replacement is a wider one: dignity and the right of every person to develop freely (1952, 2002, 2022), a duty of care to all of humanity (2002, 2022), 'helping and not harming' (2022), and the inherent worth and dignity of each person in a world free of cruelty and violence (Manifesto III).

The tensions lie in the excluded areas and in war. Manifesto II (historic) affirms a right to abortion, and the AHA, the Council for Secular Humanism (Affirmations of Humanism) and Humanists UK support dying with dignity or assisted dying: exclusions (c) and (d). On war, Manifesto II (historic) calls war obsolete, while Humanists UK's Tier 2 material says some humanists are pacifists and others accept war in self-defence or to protect lives as a last resort. Manifesto II also calls ethics 'autonomous and situational', which Article I resolved as limiting how principles are held, not whether the harm limit is held.

The bodies that apply the declarations' general principles to killing by name are modern ones, outside the corpus. Their standing: Humanists International is the body whose General Assembly adopts the declarations, so its policies are the nearest thing the tradition has to an authorised application of them. But sources/corpus.md does not list them, and the corpus rule recorded in advance keeps such material from carrying a verdict. They are:
- the General Statement of Policy, which condemns all violence except self-defence recognised by international law, and condemns terrorism;
- the Principles and Objectives of the IHEU, which state each person's right to be secure in their physical being and, on that basis, support abolishing war and the death penalty;
- the Death Penalty policy and the 1989 resolution, grounded in the right to life in UDHR Article 3;
- UN and news statements condemning honour killings, the murder of atheist writers and the targeting of civilians in war.

These do not limit, suspend or repudiate any counted text. They make explicit what the 'helping and not harming', 'worth and dignity of the individual' and 'universal legal human rights' clauses are taken to include. Under rule 1 they explain but do not decide.

K1.reason:
Counted Tier 1 texts accepted across the movement teach a wider duty that reaches every person (rule 7):
- the 2022 Declaration's 'helping and not harming', with the worth and dignity of the individual, the rights of every human, and its rejection of all prejudice (row 1);
- its duty of care to all of humanity (row 2);
- the same duty in the 2002 Declaration (row 3);
- Manifesto III's commitment to treat each person as having inherent worth and dignity, in a world free of cruelty and violence (row 4).

These reach everyone K1 protects, from birth (rule 9), whatever their faith, status or sex. No counted text was found that permits, commands or approves a private deliberate killing of any class of person, so rules 2 and 3 are not met in what was searched. Manifesto II's support for abortion falls under exclusion (c); support for dying with dignity falls under (d).

Caveats:
- No counted text names killing or self-defence. The support is Clear teaching through a wider rule, not Explicit.
- The statement closest to K1, exception included, is outside the corpus (row 11, Humanists International General Statement of Policy).
- The Council for Secular Humanism's own Tier 1 text (1980) showed no bearing in the parts seen. If that branch is separate and does not accept the 2022 Declaration, its result rests only on Implied evidence (rows 6 and 8). This branch question is open.

K3.reason:
No counted text names non-combatants or conduct in war. The current texts' wider duty reaches civilians in war: 'helping and not harming', a duty of care to all humanity and support for peace (rows 1 and 2), and Manifesto III's hope that differences be resolved without violence (row 4). Manifesto II (historic) renounces war altogether and calls it obsolete (row 14). This makes K3 taught by a wider rule (rule 7).

It is borderline for three reasons:
- the counted texts do not address conduct in war;
- humanists differ on whether war can be justified (row 16, Tier 2), so the wider rule is not read by all as forbidding all harm in war;
- the explicit condemnations of targeting civilians are outside the corpus (rows 11 and 15).

No humanist text found in any tier approves killing non-combatants.

verified.reconciliation:
Humanism has no scripture, no classical commentators and no body authorised to interpret its texts as binding. Its counted texts are short, revisable consensus statements by modern bodies, and later versions replace earlier ones. The 2022 Declaration replaced the 2002 Amsterdam Declaration, which had followed the 1952 text. The AHA has declared Manifestos I and II historic and superseded by Manifesto III. The 1996 Minimum Statement is the one text every member organisation of Humanists International must accept.

No counted text, in any tier searched, permits, commands or approves a private deliberate killing of any class of person. None makes a killing blameless because of who the victim is, and none limits protection to one community or grades it by status. So within K1 itself there is nothing for the tradition to reconcile. The principle carried through every replacement is a wider one:
- dignity and each person's right to free development (1952, 2002, 2022);
- a duty of care to all of humanity (2002, 2022);
- 'helping and not harming' (2022);
- treating each person as having inherent worth and dignity, in a world free of cruelty and violence (Manifesto III).

The hard texts, and how the tradition handles them:
1. Manifesto II (historic) recognises an individual's right to die with dignity, euthanasia and suicide, and a right to abortion. These are exclusions (d) and (c). They are not repeated in Manifesto III or the 2022 Declaration. The Council for Secular Humanism's Affirmations (Tier 2) limit the right to die to mature adults, and the European Humanist Federation (outside the corpus) stresses safeguards.
2. Manifesto II calls ethics 'autonomous and situational' (UNVERIFIED), and the 1980 Declaration rejects absolutist morality (UNVERIFIED). The same texts hold the dignity of each person central, or hold that objective standards emerge. This limits how rules are held, not what they forbid (Article I, counter-evidence M).
3. Two individuals linked to the movement, Joseph Fletcher (a Manifesto II signer and AHA Humanist of the Year 1974) and Peter Singer (Australian Humanist of the Year 2004), are reported to have argued that ending the lives of some severely disabled newborns can be justified. These are their own writings, outside the corpus and unverified in the primary text. No humanist declaration or body statement found adopts them.

The bodies that apply the Declarations to killing by name are modern ones, outside the corpus. Under rule 1 they explain the counted texts but do not decide. Humanists International, whose General Assembly adopts the Declarations, is the nearest thing to an authorised interpreter, but corpus.md does not list it as one. Its statements:
- condemn all violence except self-defence recognised by international law (General Statement of Policy, 2015);
- affirm each person's right to be secure in their physical being (Principles and Objectives);
- condemn honour killings by family members and laws that exempt the killers (2013, jointly with the Center for Inquiry; 2014);
- condemn the murder of atheist writers (2015, 2024);
- list female infanticide among human rights abuses (2015);
- condemn the indiscriminate targeting of civilians in war (2014).

These statements do not limit, suspend or repudiate any counted text. They make explicit what the non-harm, dignity and human-rights clauses are taken to include. No humanist body statement was found on the disability cases (for example the Dutch Groningen Protocol).

On war, the 1986 resolution and Humanists UK's explanatory material accept defensive force as a last resort while counting harm to the innocent against war. So Manifesto III's hope for a world 'without resorting to violence' is read by the tradition's own bodies as an aspiration, not an absolute bar, which matches K1's exception. On courts, Humanists International opposes capital punishment outright, asking more than K1.
